  "diagnosis": {
    "diagnosis": "BIOMASS_AS_READ",
    "late_biomass_estimate_od": 0.55,
    "p_biomass_above_reading": 0.15,
    "rationale": "The flat ~0.53 plateau after the steep rise up to 5 h could have meant the reader was saturating, so I diluted the retained aliquots and back-calculated. 18 h ×10: 0.0596 and 0.0554 (mean 0.0575, which works out to 0.575). 18 h ×4: 0.1337 and 0.1403 (mean 0.137, which works out to 0.548). 12 h ×10: 0.0534 and 0.0615 (mean 0.0575, which works out to 0.575). If the reader were saturated, the diluted samples would scale to much more than 0.53, and the ×4 and ×10 results would disagree, with the bigger dilution giving the higher value. Instead, both dilutions give 0.55–0.58. That is within about 5–8% of the undiluted readings and close to their hour-to-hour spread (0.50–0.55). The diluted readings are also the same at 12 h and 18 h, so the plateau looks like a real stationary phase rather than the reader hitting its limit. I can't rule out a small compression of a few percent, but there's no sign the biomass is much higher than the undiluted readings show. Best estimate of the 18 h OD is about 0.55."
  },
